      <title>UART commmuniation analyse in vivado</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/posts/uart_communication_analyse/</link>
      <pubDate>Sat, 08 Nov 2025 10:17:00 +0900</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/posts/uart_communication_analyse/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;1description&#34;&gt;1.Description&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;h1 id=&#34;2detailed-analysis&#34;&gt;2.Detailed Analysis&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;21uart_rxv&#34;&gt;2.1.uart_rx.v&lt;/h2&gt;



  




  




&lt;p&gt;&lt;details  open=true &gt;
  &lt;summary markdown=&#34;span&#34;&gt;2.1.1&lt;/summary&gt;
  &lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-v&#34; data-lang=&#34;v&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// Module: UART Receiver
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// Function: Convert serial RXD signal to 8-bit parallel data_out 
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;module&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;uart_rx&lt;/span&gt;(
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    input             clk,        
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    input             rxd,        
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    output reg [&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;7&lt;/span&gt;:&lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;]  data_out,   
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    output reg        data_valid  
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    );
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    parameter &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;CLKS_PER_BIT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;5208&lt;/span&gt;;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// Calculate (Clock Frequency / Baud Rate): For example, with FPGA clock at 50MHz and UART at 9600 baud,
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// FPGA needs 5208 clock cycles to receive one UART bit
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;    
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    localparam &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;STATE_IDLE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    localparam &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;STATE_START&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    localparam &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;STATE_DATA&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    localparam &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;STATE_STOP&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// It&amp;#39;s better to decide how many states we need.Here,We need four.
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;    reg [&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;:&lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;] state &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;STATE_IDLE&lt;/span&gt;;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// Initialize state register to hold 4 possible states (IDLE, START, DATA, STOP)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;    reg [&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;12&lt;/span&gt;:&lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;] clk_counter &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// Clock counter, must be wide enough to hold 5208 cycles (needs 13 bits)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;    reg [&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;:&lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;] bit_counter &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// Counts received data bits (0 to 7, one byte)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;    reg [&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;7&lt;/span&gt;:&lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;] data_reg &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;// Temporary register to hold incoming data bits
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/details&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Convolution and Sampling in Fourier and Laplace</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/posts/convolution_and_sampling_in_fourier_laplace/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 03 Nov 2025 10:17:00 +0900</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/posts/convolution_and_sampling_in_fourier_laplace/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;concepts-introdutions&#34;&gt;Concepts Introdutions&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;1-convolution&#34;&gt;1. Convolution&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;the process of &amp;ldquo;&lt;strong&gt;mixing&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;rdquo; or &amp;ldquo;&lt;strong&gt;modifying&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;rdquo; one signal with another.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;2-sampling&#34;&gt;2. Sampling()&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;3-fourier&#34;&gt;3. Fourier&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&#34;31fourier-series&#34;&gt;3.1.Fourier series&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A Fourier Series is a mathematical tool used to decompose any &amp;ldquo;well-behaved&amp;rdquo; periodic signal into a sum of simple, pure sinusoids (sines and cosines).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4 id=&#34;significance-why-is-it-so-important-in-engineering&#34;&gt;Significance: Why is it so important in Engineering?&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Its significance lies in its ability to translate problems from the Time Domain to the Frequency Domain.&lt;br&gt;
Time Domain (Oscilloscope View): This is how we see a signal. We see its voltage (amplitude) changing over time. This view is very poor for understanding the signal&amp;rsquo;s underlying structure.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;br&gt;
Frequency Domain (Spectrum Analyzer View): This is how we analyze a signal. The Fourier Series gives us this view. It shows us the signal&amp;rsquo;s &amp;ldquo;spectrum&amp;rdquo;—a graph of which frequencies ($n\omega_0$) are present and how strong each one ($|c_n|$) is.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
